  "query_preview": "..This morning's stratiform rain has pushed out of\nnorthern Utah and clearing skies are starting to erode the stable\nairmass that was left in place after this morning's rain.\nConvection is ongoing across portions of southeastern Utah where\nshear is maximized. 30-50 kt shear will gradually overspread the\narea and with the center of this upper level low just to our west\nthunderstorms will start to blossom this afternoon across the\nnorthern half of Utah and portions of eastern Utah. SPC has issued\na marginal risk for severe thunderstorms across this area with the\nhighest risk being for a couple of locations seeing wind gusts in\nthe 60-75 mph range in addition to some small hail, brief heavy\nrain, and frequent lightning. The storm motion will remain fast\nwith these storms which will help limit the flash flood potential\nto areas that experience training thunderstorms.\n\nGuidance pushes all of this convection out of the CWA by 04Z with\na dry and stable pattern remaining in it's wake, aside from\nsoutheastern Utah. Very seasonal conditions with near normal\ntemperatures and dry conditions will persist through the weekend\nacross a majority of Utah as tropical moisture flows into New\nMexico and Colorado. This moisture will be close enough to\nsoutheastern Utah to keep isolated diurnal convection possible\nacross that area over the next couple of days.\n\nWith a lot of tropical activity in the eastern Pacific and\nsouthwesterly flow in advance of approaching troughs we will\nneed to monitor the threat for flash flooding with our next\napproaching trough. Guidance is showing the next trough arriving\nearly next week, but ensemble members are not in agreement on the\ntiming and placement of the trough. 23% of members bring most of\nthis moisture into Utah early next week while the remaining\nmembers are a mix between keeping this moisture to the east or\nhaving a lower amount of moisture impacting the CWA.",
